  "final_text": "Both objects exist and the rule is enabled and running cleanly.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-eb897d4e Web logs overview\" (ID `d73e828d-3337-41a5-9aa4-457ccae74e8b`) has five panels:\n- A markdown header.\n- Total requests (count).\n- Unique clients (unique count of `clientip`).\n- Total bytes transferred (sum of `bytes`).\n- Requests over time (line chart on `timestamp`).\n\nI set the dashboard time range to 2026-10-01 through 2026-12-31 so the panels show the sample data.\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-eb897d4e 5xx spike\" (ID `e966fd49-522b-4393-9437-9b9d3e4c0ea3`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every 1m, counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]` over the last 5m, and fires when the count is greater than 10. It's enabled, and its last run succeeded.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- **The first rule version failed.** I used `{{date_start}}` / `{{date_end}}` placeholders, and this Kibana build didn't substitute them. I replaced them with `now-5m` / `now` and confirmed the next run succeeded.\n- **The rule is unlikely to fire on this data.** The sample has only 56 responses with 5xx codes across the past dates, about one per 5-minute window at most. Those counts reflect the sample data, not the rule's logic.\n- **Part of the sample data is in the future.** Timestamps run from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, and today is 2026-10-12. Roughly half the 5xx events are dated after today.\n- **No notification action is attached.** You didn't ask for one, so the rule records alerts but sends nothing. I can add a connector if you want notifications.\n- **Dashboard panels are my choice.** The request didn't specify the metrics, so I picked these four. I left out a 5xx count panel because this schema's metric types didn't make a filtered count easy to express.\n\nThe API reference files and my scripts are not in the project directory. The dashboard and rule JSON are in the scratchpad.",
